WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.649 --> 00:00:06.399
If you imagine a typical American city street,
and you take away the space that’s dedicated

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:09.230
to cars, you aren’t left with very much.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.929
There are some narrow walkways on the side,
and some bridges in between them, but not

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:13.900
much else.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.020
Cars dominate cities.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.950
Spend some time walking around most cities
and you’ll find yourself pushed to narrow

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.500
sidewalks, waiting for crosswalk lights.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.780
You’ll find cyclists navigating really narrow
strips of space.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.160
Americans are used to cars the way that fish
are used to water.

00:00:28.169 --> 00:00:32.640
That’s so ubiquitous in the U.S. that I
think for most people, it just never occurred

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.210
to them that it could be otherwise.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.890
But what if there were a way to change that?

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:42.000
To give space back to pedestrians and bicyclists,
and to make cities more friendly to life outside

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.100
of a car?

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.000
It turns out Barcelona might have a solution.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.260
In 2014, the city was faced with serious air
pollution problems.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.730
Barcelona and its 35 surrounding municipalities
consistently failed to meet the EU’s air

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.160
quality targets.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:02.230
Studies were showing that air pollution in
the region causes 3,500 premature deaths every

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.230
year.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.580
Traffic in the city also causes severe noise
pollution.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.170
So the city developed an extensive Urban Mobility
Plan with the hope of reducing traffic by

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.330
21 percent.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.930
The coolest part of the plan

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:13.700
were these things:

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.100
They call them "superilles".

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.120
Superilles?

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.760
“Si, superilles.”

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.610
That translates to “superblocks”.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:26.720
It’s this urban design concept intended
to minimize the presence of cars in city centers.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.960
The word “superblock” has been used before
to describe huge city blocks without any passageways

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:31.960
for cars.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.210
But that’s not what’s happening here.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.410
So here’s how Barcelona’s plan works.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.810
You take nine square city blocks and close off
the inside to through traffic.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:44.640
So buses, big freight trucks — or any vehicles
that are trying to get from one part of town

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.530
to the next — have to drive around the perimeter.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:51.229
Inside the superblock, the speed limit is
kept to 10 kilometers per hour — that translates

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.800
to just over 6 miles per hour — and curbside parking is replaced by underground parking.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.271
That means you wind up with street space for
markets, outdoor games, and events.

00:02:00.271 --> 00:02:05.790
Within this nine square block perimeter
you're gonna have kind of a pleasant streetscape

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:11.989
where people can walk around and mingle and
do things without this kind of constant fear

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:13.709
of cars around.

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:17.620
The concept is going to be tested out in five
neighborhoods, but the city has identified

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.430
120 possible intersections throughout the
region where it could be implemented.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.629
So how do we know what the results of this kind
of plan would look like?

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:29.090
Well, northwest of Barcelona is a city called
Vitoria-Gasteiz, which has implemented superblock

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.359
designs since 2008.

00:02:30.359 --> 00:02:35.069
In the main superblock at the city center,
pedestrian space increased from 45 percent

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:37.969
of the total surface area to 74 percent.

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:43.700
With so much less traffic, noise levels dropped
from 66.5 dBA to 61 dBA.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.920
Most impressive of all, there was a 42% reduction
in nitrogen oxide emissions and a 38% reduction

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.139
in particle pollution in the area.

00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:52.680
On top of that, business is up.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:58.389
What you consistently see when people change
their streetscapes to prioritize human beings

00:02:58.389 --> 00:03:03.029
over cars is you don't see any decline in
economic activity, you see the opposite.

00:03:03.029 --> 00:03:09.189
You get more people walking and cycling
around more slowly, stopping more often patronizing

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:15.900
businesses more, and that … center of social
activity will tend to build on itself.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.230
So here’s the question: could something
like this work in an American city?

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:26.549
Barcelona has some unique advantages getting
started on this plan, in that a lot of it

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:32.430
was built before cars, and a lot of it was
built on a simple grid.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:38.579
The district of Eixample — where the superblock
plan is based — was designed in 1859

00:03:38.579 --> 00:03:41.719
in this repetitive grid structure by this guy, Ildefons Cerdá.

00:03:41.719 --> 00:03:46.269
He basically invented the word for (and the study
of) “urbanization” when he laid out this

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:51.019
grid plan for Barcelona that evenly distributed
resources like schools and hospitals.

00:03:51.019 --> 00:03:54.680
But superblock designers insist
that cities don’t need a simple grid structure

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.220
to implement this kind of plan.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.160
It can work anywhere.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.760
Now, cities in the US have have attempted some
car-minimizing projects like this.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:07.079
The problem is, they’re usually done in
wealthier areas with lots of existing businesses.

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:11.730
Zoning policies often require separation of
residential and commercial areas — but an

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.669
ideal walkable area would be a mix of the
two.

00:04:14.669 --> 00:04:18.750
On top of that, zoning minimums on parking
availability encourage the presence of cars

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:24.720
and parking lots, and minimums on street width
make for wide, unwalkable streets.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.390
Because of that, walkable districts are basically
isolated luxury items in the US.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.909
What makes the Barcelona plan different is
that they aren’t setting aside one fancy

00:04:32.909 --> 00:04:35.849
neighborhood or town square to make pedestrian-friendly

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:38.040
— instead, by proposing superblocks throughout

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:42.960
the entire city, they’ve declared car-free
spaces a right for everybody, no matter what

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.960
part of town they're in.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:52.180
Maybe —&nbsp;this might be overly optimistic —&nbsp;but I think it has sunk in in the U. S. that the model whereby

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:57.140
every city resident comes with a car — and drives a car everywhere – is just inherently limited.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:01.860
It limits the growth of your city, it limits the health of your city and the growth of your city.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:09.360
So one way or another we have to find ways of having a lot of people live close to one another without all of

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.020
them having cars.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:16.940
You know, being able to get around and work and play in live and have enjoyable lives without cars.

